; Reference RTTM for `2-two-speakers-en.wav` (34.000 s, 16 kHz mono).
;
; Source of audio:
;   https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/releases/download/speaker-segmentation-models/2-two-speakers-en.wav
;   SHA-256: ee9c33d34e8f0fda4b78277f609944a1565aa16e6e2146f4cb8f0efb0d70030b
;   Published by k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx as a 2-speaker English test fixture.
;
; Provenance of these turns: independently produced by the upstream
; `sherpa-onnx-offline-speaker-diarization` CLI (sherpa-onnx v1.12.9 release
; build, --clustering.num-clusters=2, pyannote-segmentation-3-0 + nemo_en
; titanet_small) and used here as a **binding-fidelity reference**.
;
; A binding-fidelity reference does *not* claim to be human-annotated
; ground truth. Its purpose is to answer Q-P0-6 (Phase 0): does the
; `sherpa-rs 0.6.8` Rust binding expose the same diarization algorithm as
; the upstream C tool, byte-for-byte? If the spike's DER against this RTTM
; is ~0%, the binding is wiring through the underlying algorithm
; correctly; if it diverges, the binding is mis-marshalling or selecting
; a different code path.
;
; A separate "is the algorithm itself accurate against human-annotated
; truth?" question is a property of sherpa-onnx, not of the binding, and
; is out of scope for Spike 4. The algorithm is treated as a black box;
; this spike verifies the binding around it.
;
; Format: NIST md-eval RTTM v1.0
;   SPEAKER <file_id> 1 <start_s> <dur_s> <NA> <NA> <speaker> <NA> <NA>
;
